    in. Best behavior, most surface area.

  [1mMy recommendation: implicit by involvement, with an explicit mute/watch override — but build the implicit half first.[0m
  For a present, collaborating team, involvement is a genuinely good predictor, and it solves the empty-state problem
  that kills explicit-only. The override half is additive and can land in a second pass. The one thing I'd lock in now
  regardless: the task model carries [38;5;153mcreator[39m and [38;5;153massignee[39m from the start, because inferring involvement retroactively is
  the expensive mistake.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How does a user come to "care about" a task? → Implicit, override later (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Locked in. And I'll hold you to the corollary: [38;5;153mcreator[39m and [38;5;153massignee[39m become non-negotiable fields in the stage-one task
  model.

  One more product question before I put approaches in front of you — what actually counts as a change worth announcing.
  The reason this matters more than it sounds: a notification system's failure mode is almost never "missed something,"
  it's "so noisy people stopped looking." Every field you make notifiable is a chance to train users to ignore the
  bell.

  Two rules I'd apply regardless of your answer, so you can push back now: [1mnever notify you about your own action[0m, and
  [1mcollapse rapid repeated edits to the same task by the same person into one entry[0m rather than emitting five.

